{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HwAPGWL9XfxNckwaMu5r3Z","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall assessment scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information from the ACL Rolling Review's review form regarding the soundness, excitement, and overall assessment scales:\n\n**Soundness Scale:**\nThe soundness assessment uses a 5-point scale where 5 = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type,\" 4 = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims,\" and 3 = \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\" The evaluation considers whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support, and for experimental papers, the depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation.\n\n**Excitement Scale:**\nThe excitement scale also uses 5 as \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference,\" and 4 as \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\" The form notes that excitement is subjective and may relate to transformational or innovative aspects, impact on communities, or relevance to the reviewer's own research.\n\n**Overall Assessment Scale:**\nThe overall assessment includes ratings such as 5 = \"Consider for Award,\" 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle,\" 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle,\" and 1 = \"Do not resubmit.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8","uuid":"5ac20b3b-bb91-440d-bf12-e43dc5d58246","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:26:47.902Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall assessment scale","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01AW9SGVXwKkZDnQidNrnpeX","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information from the ACL Rolling Review's review form regarding the soundness, excitement, and overall assessment scales:\n\n**Soundness Scale:**\nThe soundness assessment uses a 5-point scale where 5 = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type,\" 4 = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims,\" and 3 = \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\" The evaluation considers whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, whether it clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support, and for experimental papers, the depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation.\n\n**Excitement Scale:**\nThe excitement scale also uses 5 as \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference,\" and 4 as \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\" The form notes that excitement is subjective and may relate to transformational or innovative aspects, impact on communities, or relevance to the reviewer's own research.\n\n**Overall Assessment Scale:**\nThe overall assessment includes ratings such as 5 = \"Consider for Award,\" 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle,\" 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle,\" and 1 = \"Do not resubmit.\""],"durationSeconds":5.318979888999995}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DaEqc5QUyfdSwjsb6nTiCG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form details. Let me consolidate the scoring criteria:\n\nARR Review Form (used for EMNLP 2026 via ACL Rolling Review):\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Poor\n- 1 = Major issues\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: would recommend to others\n\n\n- 4 = Exciting: would mention to others\n- 3 = Ambivalent\n- 2 = Mediocre\n- 1 = Not exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5):**\n- 5 = Consider for Award\n- 4 = Strong accept\n- 3 = Accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nI've got the full rubric now, so I'm ready to assign the two reviewers to this paper on how LLMs should generate evidence.\n\nThe work focuses on document-level relation extraction using Qwen3-4B with QLoRA fine-tuning on DocRED, examining three main angles: a task formulation with evaluation framework, comparisons across training paradigms, and analysis of evaluation metrics. The key findings show that RSFT reliably boosts evidence quality regardless of scoring approach, while GRPO causes the model to forget extraction capabilities entirely, and DPO prevents forgetting but doesn't outperform standard fine-tuning.\n\nLooking at the empirical results, the EDCR metric shows weak alignment with human judgment, the test set improvement from RSFT over SFT is marginal and not statistically significant, and there's a concerning generalization gap where RSFT drops much more sharply from dev to test than SFT does. Now I'm planning the review structure—I'll assign one reviewer to focus on methods and experimental design, another to evaluate novelty and positioning relative to prior work, then run a roundtable discussion where reviewers independently assess first, share findings, identify convergences and disagreements, and finally synthesize into a coherent evaluation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":62526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":486},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8","uuid":"069f13a4-dbdf-4107-99db-1ca21b2247c4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DaEqc5QUyfdSwjsb6nTiCG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper content, review context, and EMNLP/ARR scoring criteria. Now dispatching two parallel reviewers: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":62526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":486},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8","uuid":"c6dcdbda-f333-45cb-9bbe-b7a985146fc6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DaEqc5QUyfdSwjsb6nTiCG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Sq2QZGoDYHg1UKf13hDhjp","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission via ACL Rolling Review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Ambivalent, 2=Mediocre, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=Award, 4.5=Strong Accept, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Absolutely certain, 4=Quite sure, 3=Fairly confident, 2=Willing to defend but not sure, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"How Should LLMs Generate Evidence for Document-Level Relation Extraction? A Systematic Study of Training Paradigms and Evaluation\"\n\n**Core idea:** First systematic study of LLM-based evidence generation for DocRE. Instead of proposing a single method, frames the problem along three axes: (1) task definition + evaluation framework (conditional vs joint Evi-F1, plus EDCR metric), (2) training paradigm comparison (RSFT, GRPO, DPO), (3) evaluation metric analysis.\n\n**Base model:** Qwen3-4B with QLoRA (rank=64, α=128) on DocRED.\n\n**Methods:**\n- SFT baseline: structured output (relation type + evidence sentence indices)\n- Candidate Scoring: N=8 candidates scored by DeBERTa-v3-base NLI model\n  - Flat-NLI: mean p_pos across evidence sentences\n  - CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination): margin between gold evidence p_pos and highest-scoring entity-sharing distractor p_pos\n- RSFT: top-k=1 selection from candidates, fine-tune on selected\n- GRPO: online RL with CED reward, G=4 and G=8\n- DPO: offline preference learning from CED-ranked pairs\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:**\n- Rel-F1: standard micro-F1 over (h,r,t) triples\n- Evi-F1_cond: evidence F1 only for correctly extracted relations\n- Evi-F1_joint: evidence counts only if relation is also correct (DREEAM protocol)\n- EDCR: fraction of cited sentences that share entities with target relation but are not gold evidence (lower is better)\n\n**Key Results (all from the paper):**\n\nTable 1 (Dev set):\n- SFT: Rel-F1=40.57, Evi-F1_cond=79.79, EDCR=71.18\n- RSFT flat-NLI: 45.60, 81.20, 65.80\n- RSFT+CED (s44): **46.55**, 80.87, **63.50**\n- CED-as-reranker: 41.30, 80.20, 66.80\n- DPO+CED: 38.50, 80.50, 65.20\n\nTable 2 (Test set):\n- SFT: 39.30, —, 24.80 (joint), 73.20\n- RSFT+CED: 40.80, 40.10 (Ign-F1), 26.50 (joint), 69.00\n- DREEAM (paper): 63.00, —, 57.34 (joint), N/A\n- RSFT advantage shrinks to +1.5pp Rel-F1 (p=0.08, not significant at α=0.05)\n\nTable 3 (Dev vs Test):\n- RSFT dev→test drop: -5.75pp Rel-F1\n- SFT dev→test drop: -1.27pp Rel-F1\n\nTable 4 (CED vs flat-NLI): No statistically significant differences (p>0.10 on all three metrics). Jaccard overlap=0.76.\n\nTable 5 (NLI model ablation): DeBERTa-base vs large — no meaningful difference.\n\nTable 7 (top-k): k=1 (2718 samples) vs k=3 (8154 samples) — slight degradation with more data.\n\nTable 8 (GRPO): All variants catastrophically fail. CED G=4 lr=5e-5: Rel-F1=21.7. CED G=4 lr=5e-6: Rel-F1=1.4, Evi-F1=85.0, EDCR=98.8 (extreme decoupling). flat-NLI and extract-only G=4: collapse.\n\nTable 9 (G=4 vs G=8): G=8 partially recovers (+1.8pp Rel-F1) but still -17.1pp below SFT.\n\nTable 10 (G=8 trajectory): Reward peaks at step 80 (1.52), Rel-F1 peaks at step 80 (23.5) then declines to 19.8 at step 100.\n\nTable 11 (Online-offline spectrum): GRPO G=4 < GRPO G=8 < DPO ≈ SFT < RSFT flat-NLI < RSFT+CED\n\nTable 12 (Cross-paradigm): Joint Evi-F1: RSFT 33.8 vs DREEAM 60.8 (-27pp). Conditional Evi-F1: RSFT 80.9 vs DREEAM N/A.\n\nTable 13 (EDCR diagnostic): EDCR captures GRPO decoupling (98.8) invisible to Evi-F1 (85.0). Normal range: 63.5-71.2.\n\nTable 14 (EDCR human validation): Pearson r=0.28, Spearman ρ=0.31. Precision=0.55, Recall=0.62. Human distractor rate=0.48 vs EDCR predicted=0.65.\n\nTable 15 (EDCR disagreement): 45% false positives from incomplete gold annotations.\n\nTable 16 (Model size): 1.7B ΔRSFT=+2.6pp, 4B ΔRSFT=+6.0pp, 8B ΔRSFT=+1.7pp (non-monotonic).\n\nTable 17 (Re-DocRED): RSFT gain shrinks from +6.0pp to +2.3pp.\n\n**Limitations stated:** (1) all QLoRA, (2) GRPO limited to G≤8, (3) 100-sample human study small, (4) DocRED only.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what experiments were planned)\n\nThe full experiment design includes these planned experiments:\n1. CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation — DONE in paper (Table 4)\n2. Test set evaluation — DONE (Table 2)\n3. EDCR pipeline validation + cross-method diagnostic — DONE (Table 13)\n4. DREEAM baseline reproduction — DONE (Table 12)\n5. CED-as-reranker — DONE (Table 6)\n6. Model size ablation (1.7B/8B) — DONE (Table 16)\n7. Re-DocRED extension — DONE (Table 17)\n8. top_k ablation (k=1 vs k=3) — DONE (Table 7)\n9. GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization — DONE (Tables 8-10)\n10. NLI model ablation (base vs large) — DONE (Table 5)\n11. GRPO G=8 experiment — DONE (Table 9)\n12. DPO+CED experiment — DONE (Table 11)\n13. EDCR human annotation validation — DONE (Tables 14-15)\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Do they support the claims? Are baselines appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are results statistically significant? Are appropriate tests used?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters reported?\n4. **Methodological soundness**: Are the proposed methods (CED, EDCR, evaluation protocols) well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are results interpreted fairly? Are limitations acknowledged?\n6. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## Summary (2-3 sentences)\n## Strengths (bulleted)\n## Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n## Questions for Authors\n## Soundness: X/5\n## Excitement: X/5  \n## Overall: X/5\n## Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Judge based on what is in the paper."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":62526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":486},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8","uuid":"25504d29-bc17-476a-803a-3a245b1acc3d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aebcf4b7d1ca414e2","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sq2QZGoDYHg1UKf13hDhjp","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission via ACL Rolling Review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Ambivalent, 2=Mediocre, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=Award, 4.5=Strong Accept, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Absolutely certain, 4=Quite sure, 3=Fairly confident, 2=Willing to defend but not sure, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"How Should LLMs Generate Evidence for Document-Level Relation Extraction? A Systematic Study of Training Paradigms and Evaluation\"\n\n**Core idea:** First systematic study of LLM-based evidence generation for DocRE. Instead of proposing a single method, frames the problem along three axes: (1) task definition + evaluation framework (conditional vs joint Evi-F1, plus EDCR metric), (2) training paradigm comparison (RSFT, GRPO, DPO), (3) evaluation metric analysis.\n\n**Base model:** Qwen3-4B with QLoRA (rank=64, α=128) on DocRED.\n\n**Methods:**\n- SFT baseline: structured output (relation type + evidence sentence indices)\n- Candidate Scoring: N=8 candidates scored by DeBERTa-v3-base NLI model\n  - Flat-NLI: mean p_pos across evidence sentences\n  - CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination): margin between gold evidence p_pos and highest-scoring entity-sharing distractor p_pos\n- RSFT: top-k=1 selection from candidates, fine-tune on selected\n- GRPO: online RL with CED reward, G=4 and G=8\n- DPO: offline preference learning from CED-ranked pairs\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:**\n- Rel-F1: standard micro-F1 over (h,r,t) triples\n- Evi-F1_cond: evidence F1 only for correctly extracted relations\n- Evi-F1_joint: evidence counts only if relation is also correct (DREEAM protocol)\n- EDCR: fraction of cited sentences that share entities with target relation but are not gold evidence (lower is better)\n\n**Key Results (all from the paper):**\n\nTable 1 (Dev set):\n- SFT: Rel-F1=40.57, Evi-F1_cond=79.79, EDCR=71.18\n- RSFT flat-NLI: 45.60, 81.20, 65.80\n- RSFT+CED (s44): **46.55**, 80.87, **63.50**\n- CED-as-reranker: 41.30, 80.20, 66.80\n- DPO+CED: 38.50, 80.50, 65.20\n\nTable 2 (Test set):\n- SFT: 39.30, —, 24.80 (joint), 73.20\n- RSFT+CED: 40.80, 40.10 (Ign-F1), 26.50 (joint), 69.00\n- DREEAM (paper): 63.00, —, 57.34 (joint), N/A\n- RSFT advantage shrinks to +1.5pp Rel-F1 (p=0.08, not significant at α=0.05)\n\nTable 3 (Dev vs Test):\n- RSFT dev→test drop: -5.75pp Rel-F1\n- SFT dev→test drop: -1.27pp Rel-F1\n\nTable 4 (CED vs flat-NLI): No statistically significant differences (p>0.10 on all three metrics). Jaccard overlap=0.76.\n\nTable 5 (NLI model ablation): DeBERTa-base vs large — no meaningful difference.\n\nTable 7 (top-k): k=1 (2718 samples) vs k=3 (8154 samples) — slight degradation with more data.\n\nTable 8 (GRPO): All variants catastrophically fail. CED G=4 lr=5e-5: Rel-F1=21.7. CED G=4 lr=5e-6: Rel-F1=1.4, Evi-F1=85.0, EDCR=98.8 (extreme decoupling). flat-NLI and extract-only G=4: collapse.\n\nTable 9 (G=4 vs G=8): G=8 partially recovers (+1.8pp Rel-F1) but still -17.1pp below SFT.\n\nTable 10 (G=8 trajectory): Reward peaks at step 80 (1.52), Rel-F1 peaks at step 80 (23.5) then declines to 19.8 at step 100.\n\nTable 11 (Online-offline spectrum): GRPO G=4 < GRPO G=8 < DPO ≈ SFT < RSFT flat-NLI < RSFT+CED\n\nTable 12 (Cross-paradigm): Joint Evi-F1: RSFT 33.8 vs DREEAM 60.8 (-27pp). Conditional Evi-F1: RSFT 80.9 vs DREEAM N/A.\n\nTable 13 (EDCR diagnostic): EDCR captures GRPO decoupling (98.8) invisible to Evi-F1 (85.0). Normal range: 63.5-71.2.\n\nTable 14 (EDCR human validation): Pearson r=0.28, Spearman ρ=0.31. Precision=0.55, Recall=0.62. Human distractor rate=0.48 vs EDCR predicted=0.65.\n\nTable 15 (EDCR disagreement): 45% false positives from incomplete gold annotations.\n\nTable 16 (Model size): 1.7B ΔRSFT=+2.6pp, 4B ΔRSFT=+6.0pp, 8B ΔRSFT=+1.7pp (non-monotonic).\n\nTable 17 (Re-DocRED): RSFT gain shrinks from +6.0pp to +2.3pp.\n\n**Limitations stated:** (1) all QLoRA, (2) GRPO limited to G≤8, (3) 100-sample human study small, (4) DocRED only.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what experiments were planned)\n\nThe full experiment design includes these planned experiments:\n1. CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation — DONE in paper (Table 4)\n2. Test set evaluation — DONE (Table 2)\n3. EDCR pipeline validation + cross-method diagnostic — DONE (Table 13)\n4. DREEAM baseline reproduction — DONE (Table 12)\n5. CED-as-reranker — DONE (Table 6)\n6. Model size ablation (1.7B/8B) — DONE (Table 16)\n7. Re-DocRED extension — DONE (Table 17)\n8. top_k ablation (k=1 vs k=3) — DONE (Table 7)\n9. GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization — DONE (Tables 8-10)\n10. NLI model ablation (base vs large) — DONE (Table 5)\n11. GRPO G=8 experiment — DONE (Table 9)\n12. DPO+CED experiment — DONE (Table 11)\n13. EDCR human annotation validation — DONE (Tables 14-15)\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Do they support the claims? Are baselines appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are results statistically significant? Are appropriate tests used?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters reported?\n4. **Methodological soundness**: Are the proposed methods (CED, EDCR, evaluation protocols) well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are results interpreted fairly? Are limitations acknowledged?\n6. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## Summary (2-3 sentences)\n## Strengths (bulleted)\n## Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n## Questions for Authors\n## Soundness: X/5\n## Excitement: X/5  \n## Overall: X/5\n## Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Judge based on what is in the paper.","uuid":"30912572-e8ad-4751-9850-a1c8bc7b1329","session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission via ACL Rolling Review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Ambivalent, 2=Mediocre, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=Award, 4.5=Strong Accept, 4=Accept, 3.5=Leaning Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Absolutely certain, 4=Quite sure, 3=Fairly confident, 2=Willing to defend but not sure, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"How Should LLMs Generate Evidence for Document-Level Relation Extraction? A Systematic Study of Training Paradigms and Evaluation\"\n\n**Core idea:** First systematic study of LLM-based evidence generation for DocRE. Instead of proposing a single method, frames the problem along three axes: (1) task definition + evaluation framework (conditional vs joint Evi-F1, plus EDCR metric), (2) training paradigm comparison (RSFT, GRPO, DPO), (3) evaluation metric analysis.\n\n**Base model:** Qwen3-4B with QLoRA (rank=64, α=128) on DocRED.\n\n**Methods:**\n- SFT baseline: structured output (relation type + evidence sentence indices)\n- Candidate Scoring: N=8 candidates scored by DeBERTa-v3-base NLI model\n  - Flat-NLI: mean p_pos across evidence sentences\n  - CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination): margin between gold evidence p_pos and highest-scoring entity-sharing distractor p_pos\n- RSFT: top-k=1 selection from candidates, fine-tune on selected\n- GRPO: online RL with CED reward, G=4 and G=8\n- DPO: offline preference learning from CED-ranked pairs\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:**\n- Rel-F1: standard micro-F1 over (h,r,t) triples\n- Evi-F1_cond: evidence F1 only for correctly extracted relations\n- Evi-F1_joint: evidence counts only if relation is also correct (DREEAM protocol)\n- EDCR: fraction of cited sentences that share entities with target relation but are not gold evidence (lower is better)\n\n**Key Results (all from the paper):**\n\nTable 1 (Dev set):\n- SFT: Rel-F1=40.57, Evi-F1_cond=79.79, EDCR=71.18\n- RSFT flat-NLI: 45.60, 81.20, 65.80\n- RSFT+CED (s44): **46.55**, 80.87, **63.50**\n- CED-as-reranker: 41.30, 80.20, 66.80\n- DPO+CED: 38.50, 80.50, 65.20\n\nTable 2 (Test set):\n- SFT: 39.30, —, 24.80 (joint), 73.20\n- RSFT+CED: 40.80, 40.10 (Ign-F1), 26.50 (joint), 69.00\n- DREEAM (paper): 63.00, —, 57.34 (joint), N/A\n- RSFT advantage shrinks to +1.5pp Rel-F1 (p=0.08, not significant at α=0.05)\n\nTable 3 (Dev vs Test):\n- RSFT dev→test drop: -5.75pp Rel-F1\n- SFT dev→test drop: -1.27pp Rel-F1\n\nTable 4 (CED vs flat-NLI): No statistically significant differences (p>0.10 on all three metrics). Jaccard overlap=0.76.\n\nTable 5 (NLI model ablation): DeBERTa-base vs large — no meaningful difference.\n\nTable 7 (top-k): k=1 (2718 samples) vs k=3 (8154 samples) — slight degradation with more data.\n\nTable 8 (GRPO): All variants catastrophically fail. CED G=4 lr=5e-5: Rel-F1=21.7. CED G=4 lr=5e-6: Rel-F1=1.4, Evi-F1=85.0, EDCR=98.8 (extreme decoupling). flat-NLI and extract-only G=4: collapse.\n\nTable 9 (G=4 vs G=8): G=8 partially recovers (+1.8pp Rel-F1) but still -17.1pp below SFT.\n\nTable 10 (G=8 trajectory): Reward peaks at step 80 (1.52), Rel-F1 peaks at step 80 (23.5) then declines to 19.8 at step 100.\n\nTable 11 (Online-offline spectrum): GRPO G=4 < GRPO G=8 < DPO ≈ SFT < RSFT flat-NLI < RSFT+CED\n\nTable 12 (Cross-paradigm): Joint Evi-F1: RSFT 33.8 vs DREEAM 60.8 (-27pp). Conditional Evi-F1: RSFT 80.9 vs DREEAM N/A.\n\nTable 13 (EDCR diagnostic): EDCR captures GRPO decoupling (98.8) invisible to Evi-F1 (85.0). Normal range: 63.5-71.2.\n\nTable 14 (EDCR human validation): Pearson r=0.28, Spearman ρ=0.31. Precision=0.55, Recall=0.62. Human distractor rate=0.48 vs EDCR predicted=0.65.\n\nTable 15 (EDCR disagreement): 45% false positives from incomplete gold annotations.\n\nTable 16 (Model size): 1.7B ΔRSFT=+2.6pp, 4B ΔRSFT=+6.0pp, 8B ΔRSFT=+1.7pp (non-monotonic).\n\nTable 17 (Re-DocRED): RSFT gain shrinks from +6.0pp to +2.3pp.\n\n**Limitations stated:** (1) all QLoRA, (2) GRPO limited to G≤8, (3) 100-sample human study small, (4) DocRED only.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what experiments were planned)\n\nThe full experiment design includes these planned experiments:\n1. CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation — DONE in paper (Table 4)\n2. Test set evaluation — DONE (Table 2)\n3. EDCR pipeline validation + cross-method diagnostic — DONE (Table 13)\n4. DREEAM baseline reproduction — DONE (Table 12)\n5. CED-as-reranker — DONE (Table 6)\n6. Model size ablation (1.7B/8B) — DONE (Table 16)\n7. Re-DocRED extension — DONE (Table 17)\n8. top_k ablation (k=1 vs k=3) — DONE (Table 7)\n9. GRPO forgetting analysis + visualization — DONE (Tables 8-10)\n10. NLI model ablation (base vs large) — DONE (Table 5)\n11. GRPO G=8 experiment — DONE (Table 9)\n12. DPO+CED experiment — DONE (Table 11)\n13. EDCR human annotation validation — DONE (Tables 14-15)\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Do they support the claims? Are baselines appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are results statistically significant? Are appropriate tests used?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters reported?\n4. **Methodological soundness**: Are the proposed methods (CED, EDCR, evaluation protocols) well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are results interpreted fairly? Are limitations acknowledged?\n6. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## Summary (2-3 sentences)\n## Strengths (bulleted)\n## Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n## Questions for Authors\n## Soundness: X/5\n## Excitement: X/5  \n## Overall: X/5\n## Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Judge based on what is in the paper."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sq2QZGoDYHg1UKf13hDhjp","session_id":"5167ddfd-d536-4198-9738-76cb8f0f50e8","uuid":"9fc998e5-26ab-4c73-87f8-586386e9c302","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:28:02.317Z"}
